# October – December.
DATA = [
('10-01', 'Saint Thérèse of the Child Jesus, virgin and doctor', 'Saint Thérèse of the Child Jesus', '1873, Alençon, France', '1897, Lisieux, France', ['missions', 'florists'],
 'Thérèse entered the Carmel of Lisieux at fifteen and died of tuberculosis at twenty-four. Her "little way" teaches that holiness lies in doing small things with great love and trusting God like a child. Her autobiography, *Story of a Soul*, has touched millions.\n\nThough she never left her convent, she is patron of missions and a Doctor of the Church.'),
('10-02', 'Guardian Angels', 'The Holy Guardian Angels', '', '', [],
 'The Church teaches that each person has a guardian angel to protect and guide them. Jesus said of little ones: "Their angels in heaven always behold the face of my Father."\n\nWe thank God for this gift and ask our angels\' help.'),
('10-04', 'Saint Francis of Assisi', 'Saint Francis of Assisi', '1181, Assisi, Italy', '1226, Assisi', ['animals', 'ecology', 'Italy'],
 'Francis, son of a rich merchant, heard Christ say from a crucifix, "Rebuild my Church." He gave up everything to live in poverty, joy and love for all creation. He founded the Franciscans, and two years before his death he received the stigmata.\n\nHis *Canticle of the Creatures* praises God for "Brother Sun" and "Sister Moon".'),
('10-05', 'Saint Maria Faustina Kowalska, virgin', 'Saint Faustina Kowalska', '1905, Poland', '1938, Kraków, Poland', ['Divine Mercy'],
 'Faustina was a Polish sister who received messages from Jesus about his Divine Mercy. Her diary gave the Church the Divine Mercy image, the Chaplet and Divine Mercy Sunday. Jesus asked that the image bear the words "Jesus, I trust in you."'),
('10-06', 'Saint Bruno, priest', 'Saint Bruno', 'c. 1030, Cologne, Germany', '1101, Calabria, Italy', [],
 'Bruno was a respected teacher who left the world to found the Carthusians in the mountains of the Chartreuse, a life of silence, solitude and prayer.'),
('10-07', 'Our Lady of the Rosary', 'Our Lady of the Rosary', '', '', [],
 'Pope Pius V set this feast after the victory at Lepanto in 1571, which was credited to Christians praying the Rosary. The Rosary leads us to meditate on the life of Christ with Mary.\n\nOctober is dedicated to the Holy Rosary.'),
('10-09', 'Saint Denis and companions, martyrs', 'Saint Denis and Companions', '', 'c. 250, Paris', ['France', 'Paris'],
 'Denis was a missionary bishop sent to Gaul who became the first Bishop of Paris. He was beheaded with two companions on the hill now called Montmartre.'),
('10-09', 'Saint John Leonardi, priest', 'Saint John Leonardi', '1541, Italy', '1609, Rome', [],
 'John Leonardi, a pharmacist who became a priest, founded a congregation to teach the faith and helped start the college that trains missionaries for the Church.'),
('10-11', 'Saint John XXIII, pope', 'Saint John XXIII', '1881, Sotto il Monte, Italy', '1963, Rome', ['papal delegates'],
 'Angelo Roncalli, a farmer\'s son, served as a papal diplomat before becoming pope at seventy-six. Known as "Good Pope John" for his warmth, he opened the Second Vatican Council on 11 October 1962 to renew the Church.'),
('10-14', 'Saint Callistus I, pope and martyr', 'Saint Callistus I', '', 'c. 222, Rome', [],
 'Callistus was a former slave who was put in charge of a Christian cemetery in Rome, now named after him, and later became pope. He showed mercy to repentant sinners and died a martyr.'),
('10-15', 'Saint Teresa of Jesus, virgin and doctor', 'Saint Teresa of Avila', '1515, Avila, Spain', '1582, Alba de Tormes, Spain', ['Spain', 'headache sufferers'],
 'Teresa was a Carmelite nun who reformed her order, founding many convents of strict prayer and poverty with Saint John of the Cross. Her books, such as *The Interior Castle*, are classics of prayer. "Let nothing disturb you... God alone suffices."\n\nShe was the first woman named a Doctor of the Church.'),
('10-16', 'Saint Hedwig, religious', 'Saint Hedwig', '1174, Bavaria', '1243, Silesia', [],
 'Hedwig was Duchess of Silesia, wife and mother, who founded hospitals and monasteries and served the poor. After her husband died she lived in a convent.'),
('10-16', 'Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque, virgin', 'Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque', '1647, France', '1690, Paray-le-Monial, France', [],
 'Margaret Mary was a Visitation nun to whom Jesus revealed his Sacred Heart, burning with love for humanity. She spread the devotion of the First Fridays and the feast of the Sacred Heart.'),
('10-17', 'Saint Ignatius of Antioch, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Ignatius of Antioch', '', 'c. 107, Rome', [],
 'Ignatius was Bishop of Antioch, perhaps a disciple of Saint John. Taken to Rome to be thrown to the lions, he wrote seven letters to the churches on unity and the Eucharist, calling himself "God\'s wheat, ground by the teeth of beasts". He was the first to call the Church "Catholic".'),
('10-18', 'Saint Luke the Evangelist', 'Saint Luke the Evangelist', 'Antioch', '1st century', ['doctors', 'artists'],
 'Luke was a physician and companion of Saint Paul. He wrote the Gospel that shows Jesus\' mercy for the poor and sinners, and the Acts of the Apostles. His Gospel gives us the stories of the Annunciation, the Nativity and the Prodigal Son.'),
('10-19', 'Saint Paul of the Cross, priest', 'Saint Paul of the Cross', '1694, Italy', '1775, Rome', [],
 'Paul founded the Passionists, who preach the love of God shown in the Passion of Jesus.'),
('10-19', 'Saints Jean de Brébeuf, Isaac Jogues, priests and martyrs; and their companions, martyrs', 'Saints John de Brébeuf, Isaac Jogues and Companions', '', '1642–1649, North America', ['North America'],
 'Jean de Brébeuf, Isaac Jogues and six companions were French Jesuit missionaries and lay helpers who brought the Gospel to the native peoples of North America and were martyred there.'),
('10-22', 'Saint John Paul II, pope', 'Saint John Paul II', '1920, Wadowice, Poland', '2005, Rome', ['families', 'World Youth Day'],
 'Karol Wojtyła, actor, priest and Archbishop of Kraków, became pope in 1978. "Be not afraid!" he told the world. He travelled to over 120 countries, including Nigeria in 1982 and 1998, when he beatified Cyprian Michael Tansi. He began World Youth Day and taught tirelessly on human dignity and the family.\n\nHe was canonised in 2014.'),
('10-23', 'Saint John of Capistrano, priest', 'Saint John of Capistrano', '1386, Italy', '1456, Croatia', ['military chaplains'],
 'John was a lawyer and governor who became a Franciscan preacher across Europe. At seventy he helped lead the defence of Belgrade in 1456.'),
('10-24', 'Saint Anthony Mary Claret, bishop', 'Saint Anthony Mary Claret', '1807, Spain', '1870, France', ['the Catholic press'],
 'Anthony Mary Claret was a Spanish missionary who preached widely, founded the Claretians and served as Archbishop of Santiago in Cuba. He used the printing press to spread the faith.'),
('10-28', 'Saint Simon and Saint Jude, apostles', 'Saints Simon and Jude, Apostles', '', '1st century', ['desperate causes'],
 'Simon "the Zealot" and Jude, also called Thaddeus, were among the Twelve. Tradition says they preached together and were martyred. Saint Jude is invoked in desperate causes.'),
('11-01', 'All Saints', 'All Saints', '', '', [],
 'Today we celebrate all the saints in heaven, known and unknown, the "great multitude which no one could count". They are our family and our friends, who pray for us.\n\nThe feast reminds us that we too are called to holiness.'),
('11-03', 'Saint Martin de Porres, religious', 'Saint Martin de Porres', '1579, Lima, Peru', '1639, Lima', ['racial harmony', 'social justice'],
 'Martin was born in Lima to a Spanish father and a freed African mother and suffered prejudice for his mixed race. As a Dominican brother he cared for the sick and poor of every race, and even animals, with great humility.\n\nHe is patron of racial harmony.'),
('11-04', 'Saint Charles Borromeo, bishop', 'Saint Charles Borromeo', '1538, Italy', '1584, Milan, Italy', ['seminarians', 'catechists'],
 'Charles was Archbishop of Milan who put the reforms of the Council of Trent into practice, founding seminaries and teaching the faith. During the plague he stayed with his people, caring for the sick.'),
('11-10', 'Saint Leo the Great, pope and doctor', 'Saint Leo the Great', '', '461, Rome', [],
 'Pope Leo I taught clearly that Christ is one Person, truly God and truly man, a teaching confirmed by the Council of Chalcedon. He persuaded Attila the Hun not to attack Rome.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('11-11', 'Saint Martin of Tours, bishop', 'Saint Martin of Tours', 'c. 316, Pannonia', '397, Gaul', ['soldiers', 'the poor'],
 'As a young Roman soldier, Martin cut his cloak in half to share it with a freezing beggar; that night he dreamed of Christ wearing it. He became a monk and Bishop of Tours, loved for his care of the poor.'),
('11-12', 'Saint Josaphat, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Josaphat', '1580, Ukraine', '1623, Belarus', ['Christian unity'],
 'Josaphat was a Ukrainian archbishop of the Byzantine rite who worked for unity between Eastern Christians and Rome. He was killed by opponents of that unity.'),
('11-15', 'Saint Albert the Great, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Albert the Great', 'c. 1200, Germany', '1280, Cologne', ['scientists'],
 'Albert was a Dominican scholar who studied nature, science and philosophy and taught Saint Thomas Aquinas. He served briefly as a bishop.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church and patron of scientists.'),
('11-16', 'Saint Gertrude the Great, virgin', 'Saint Gertrude the Great', '1256, Germany', 'c. 1302, Helfta, Germany', [],
 'Gertrude was a nun of Helfta in Germany whose mystical writings speak of the love of the Sacred Heart of Jesus. She is one of the few women saints called "the Great".'),
('11-16', 'Saint Margaret of Scotland', 'Saint Margaret of Scotland', 'c. 1045, Hungary', '1093, Edinburgh, Scotland', ['Scotland'],
 'Margaret was Queen of Scotland, wife and mother of eight. She used her position to serve the poor, encourage learning and renew the Church, and every day fed orphans and the needy.'),
('11-17', 'Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, religious', 'Saint Elizabeth of Hungary', '1207, Hungary', '1231, Marburg, Germany', ['hospitals', 'bakers'],
 'Elizabeth, a princess, married young and was widowed at twenty. She gave her wealth to the poor, built a hospital and served the sick herself as a Franciscan tertiary. She died at twenty-four.'),
('11-21', 'Presentation of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Presentation of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', [],
 'According to ancient tradition, Mary was presented in the Temple as a child and dedicated to God by her parents, Joachim and Anne. The feast celebrates her total gift of herself to the Lord.'),
('11-22', 'Saint Cecilia', 'Saint Cecilia', 'Rome', '3rd century, Rome', ['musicians', 'singers'],
 'Cecilia was a Roman martyr who, it is said, sang to God in her heart on her wedding day. She is named in the Roman Canon and is patron of music and musicians.'),
('11-23', 'Saint Clement I, pope and martyr', 'Saint Clement I', '', 'c. 100', [],
 'Clement was one of the first popes after Saint Peter. His letter to the Church in Corinth, urging peace and unity, is one of the earliest Christian writings after the New Testament.'),
('11-23', 'Saint Columban, religious', 'Saint Columban', 'c. 543, Ireland', '615, Bobbio, Italy', [],
 'Columban was an Irish monk who founded monasteries in France, Switzerland and Italy, renewing faith across Europe.'),
('11-24', 'Saint Andrew Dung-Lac and his companions, martyrs', 'Saints Andrew Dung-Lac and Companions', '', '1745–1862, Vietnam', ['Vietnam'],
 'Andrew Dung-Lac was a Vietnamese priest martyred in 1839. He is honoured with 116 companions, priests, religious and lay people, who died for Christ in Vietnam.'),
('11-25', 'Saint Catherine of Alexandria', 'Saint Catherine of Alexandria', 'Alexandria, Egypt', 'c. 305', ['philosophers', 'students'],
 'Catherine was a learned young woman of Alexandria in Egypt who, according to tradition, defended the faith before philosophers and converted many. She was martyred, condemned to the wheel and then beheaded.'),
('11-30', 'Saint Andrew the Apostle', 'Saint Andrew the Apostle', 'Bethsaida, Galilee', '1st century, Patras, Greece', ['fishermen', 'Scotland'],
 'Andrew, a fisherman and disciple of John the Baptist, was the first to follow Jesus. He brought his brother Simon Peter to the Lord, saying: "We have found the Messiah."\n\nTradition says he was crucified on an X-shaped cross in Greece.'),
('12-03', 'Saint Francis Xavier, priest', 'Saint Francis Xavier', '1506, Navarre, Spain', '1552, Shangchuan Island, China', ['missions'],
 'Francis Xavier was one of the first Jesuits. He carried the Gospel to India, Southeast Asia and Japan, baptising tens of thousands, and died hoping to enter China.\n\nHe is patron of missions with Saint Thérèse.'),
('12-04', 'Saint John Damascene, priest and doctor', 'Saint John Damascene', 'c. 675, Damascus', '749, near Jerusalem', [],
 'John of Damascus was a monk near Jerusalem who defended the veneration of holy images and wrote a summary of the faith.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('12-06', 'Saint Nicholas, bishop', 'Saint Nicholas', 'Patara, Asia Minor', 'c. 343, Myra', ['children', 'sailors'],
 'Nicholas was Bishop of Myra in today\'s Turkey, famous for his secret gifts to the poor. Tradition tells that he gave gold so three poor girls could marry.\n\nHe inspired the figure of Santa Claus.'),
('12-07', 'Saint Ambrose, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Ambrose', 'c. 340, Trier', '397, Milan, Italy', ['beekeepers'],
 'Ambrose was governor of Milan, not yet baptised, when the people acclaimed him bishop. He became a great pastor and teacher, wrote hymns and baptised Saint Augustine.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('12-08', 'Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'The Immaculate Conception of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', ['Nigeria', 'the United States'],
 'From the first moment of her conception, Mary was preserved free from original sin by the merits of her Son. Pope Pius IX defined this dogma in 1854; at Lourdes in 1858 Mary named herself "the Immaculate Conception".\n\nMary is the patroness of Nigeria, entrusted to her as Queen of Nigeria.'),
('12-09', 'Saint Juan Diego', 'Saint Juan Diego', '1474, Mexico', '1548, Mexico', ['indigenous peoples'],
 'Juan Diego was an indigenous man of Mexico to whom the Virgin Mary appeared at Tepeyac in 1531. Her image miraculously appeared on his cloak, the *tilma*.\n\nHe was the first indigenous saint of the Americas.'),
('12-10', 'Our Lady of Loreto', 'Our Lady of Loreto', '', '', ['aviators', 'air travellers'],
 'At Loreto in Italy, the Holy House, venerated as the home of the Holy Family in Nazareth, has drawn pilgrims for centuries. Pope Francis added this memorial in 2019.\n\nOur Lady of Loreto is patron of air travellers.'),
('12-11', 'Saint Damasus I, pope', 'Saint Damasus I', 'c. 305, Rome', '384, Rome', [],
 'Pope Damasus encouraged Saint Jerome to translate the Bible into Latin and honoured the martyrs\' tombs with inscriptions.'),
('12-12', 'Our Lady of Guadalupe', 'Our Lady of Guadalupe', '', '', ['the Americas', 'the unborn'],
 'In 1531 Mary appeared to Saint Juan Diego near Mexico City as a young indigenous woman. Her image on his cloak led millions to faith.\n\nShe is patron of the Americas and of the unborn.'),
('12-13', 'Saint Lucy of Syracuse, virgin and martyr', 'Saint Lucy', 'Syracuse, Sicily', '304, Syracuse', ['the blind', 'eye illnesses'],
 'Lucy was a young Christian woman of Sicily who gave her dowry to the poor and was martyred during the persecution of Diocletian. Her name means "light". She is named in the Roman Canon.'),
('12-14', 'Saint John of the Cross, priest and doctor', 'Saint John of the Cross', '1542, Spain', '1591, Úbeda, Spain', ['mystics'],
 'John helped Saint Teresa of Avila reform the Carmelites and was imprisoned by his own brothers for it. In prison he wrote poems of deep love for God. His *Dark Night of the Soul* guides souls through trials toward union with God.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('12-21', 'Saint Peter Canisius, priest and doctor', 'Saint Peter Canisius', '1521, Netherlands', '1597, Switzerland', ['Germany'],
 'Peter Canisius was a Dutch Jesuit who renewed the Catholic faith in Germany after the Reformation. His catechisms were used for centuries.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('12-23', 'Saint John of Kanty, priest', 'Saint John of Kanty', '1390, Poland', '1473, Kraków, Poland', ['students', 'teachers'],
 'John of Kanty was a professor at the University of Kraków, known for his learning and his generosity to poor students.'),
('12-26', 'Saint Stephen, the first martyr', 'Saint Stephen, the First Martyr', '', 'c. 34, Jerusalem', ['deacons'],
 'Stephen was one of the first seven deacons, "full of faith and the Holy Spirit". Stoned to death in Jerusalem, he prayed, "Lord, do not hold this sin against them."\n\nSaul, the future Saint Paul, watched his death.'),
('12-27', 'Saint John the Apostle and evangelist', 'Saint John, Apostle and Evangelist', 'Galilee', 'c. 100, Ephesus', ['friendship', 'writers'],
 'John, the "disciple whom Jesus loved", stood at the cross and was given Mary as his mother. He wrote the fourth Gospel, three letters and, by tradition, the Book of Revelation. "God is love."\n\nHe is said to have died in old age at Ephesus.'),
('12-28', 'Holy Innocents, martyrs', 'The Holy Innocents', '', 'Bethlehem', ['children', 'the unborn'],
 'King Herod, fearing the newborn King, ordered the killing of all boys under two in Bethlehem. The Church honours these children as martyrs who died in place of Christ.\n\nWe pray for all children who suffer violence.'),
('12-29', 'Saint Thomas Becket, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Thomas Becket', '1118, London', '1170, Canterbury, England', [],
 'Thomas was Chancellor of England and friend of King Henry II. As Archbishop of Canterbury he defended the freedom of the Church against the king and was murdered in his cathedral.'),
('12-31', 'Saint Sylvester I, pope', 'Saint Sylvester I', '', '335, Rome', [],
 'Sylvester was pope when Emperor Constantine ended the persecution of Christians. During his time the Council of Nicaea (325) proclaimed the faith in Christ, true God, and great churches like the Lateran Basilica were built.'),
]
